ATTACK 1 — "migration only ever rewrites data files, never executables or
directories":
Traced every call site of filePerm (grep across internal/services/storage):
encryptFileWithRecipient, decryptFileWithIdentity,
rollbackEncryptionWithIdentity, rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient — the four
migration helpers, all in migration.go. Their path lists come from:
 - EnableEncryptionWithProvider's filepath.Walk: skips info.IsDir(), skips
   shouldSkipEncryption (marker/verify/config files, /cache/ paths), keeps
   only ext == ".csv" || ".json". Directories are never passed to
   encryptFileWithRecipient/atomicWrite.
 - DisableEncryption's filepath.Walk: skips info.IsDir(), and appends any
   non-dir file whose content matches isAgeEncrypted(data) -- NOT
   extension-filtered and NOT gated by shouldSkipEncryption. This is a real
   asymmetry: verifyFile (.encryption-verify, an encryption-state marker,
   not a CSV/JSON data file) is itself genuine age-ciphertext written by
   EnableEncryptionWithProvider, so it gets swept into filesToDecrypt and is
   read/decrypted/re-written via atomicWrite+filePerm before being removed
   a few lines later by `os.Remove(verifyPath)`. So "(CSVs, JSON sidecars)"
   is not a literally exhaustive enumeration of what passes through
   filePerm/atomicWrite -- verifyFile does too.
   This does NOT, however, refute the comment's operative claim, which is
   about executables and directories being the only place special bits
   carry meaning: verifyFile is written via os.WriteFile(verifyPath,
   encrypted, 0644) (no special bits, ever) and is not a directory or
   executable, so this asymmetry supplies no counterexample to the actual
   ruling being defended. Recorded as a precision nit, not a refutation.
 - filepath.Walk does recurse into subdirectories (e.g. hypothetical nested
   data/settings/*.csv) but only ever hands non-dir file paths to
   atomicWrite; the directory entries themselves are never chmod'd/written.
   No counterexample: "never rewrites... directories" holds.
 - Checked whether a setgid *directory* could make atomicWrite's rename
   behave as if it preserved a special bit the comment denies: setgid on a
   directory only affects inherited *group ownership* of new entries (and
   setgid propagation to new subdirectories), not the *mode bits* of a new
   regular file, which are set explicitly and exclusively by
   os.Chmod(tmpPath, perm) in atomicWrite using filePerm's Perm()-only
   value. No interaction found that contradicts the comment.
No executable-with-meaningful-special-bit or directory counterexample found
under normal operation. The verifyFile finding is real but does not carry
special-bit meaning, so it doesn't break the claim being certified.

ATTACK 2 — "does not thread them through to atomicWrite":
Grep of every atomicWrite call fed by filePerm (migration.go:333, 378, 422,
497) confirms each passes exactly the `perm` returned by filePerm (i.e.
Mode().Perm(), 0777 bits only) -- no wider FileMode value is ever
constructed or passed. filePerm's body: `return info.Mode().Perm(), nil`
strips ModeSetuid/ModeSetgid/ModeSticky by construction (Perm() masks to
os.ModePerm = 0777). Wording check: the added comment explicitly frames
this as "a decision (ruling 2026-08-26c), not an oversight," correctly
attributing the limitation to filePerm's choice rather than implying
atomicWrite/os.Chmod can't accept special bits (it can -- os.Chmod on Unix
does translate ModeSetuid/ModeSetgid/ModeSticky to real chmod bits since Go
1.10). No misleading wording found.

